; ===============  Silly Shenanigans

; 1. Bashrc backdoor
; lets say you have access to victim machine
; Look into startup scripts; like .bashrc runs at startup
; inject it with your script,
; make him run it and write you output in shared place you have access to


; Sniffing Process Arguments
;
; ps aux
; Users forget one or more of command arguments with sensitive args
; I'm not sure if this important, but this is misconfiguration


; Snooping on Configurations
;
; Again, misconfiguration.
; Even without making mistakes, users might inadvertently leave themselves at
; risk. For example, many files in a typical user's home directory are
; world-readable by default, despite frequently being used to store sensitive
; information. Believe it or not, your .bashrc is world-readable unless you
; explicitly change it!

